  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday morning through next Monday)\nIssued at 304 PM EDT Tue Sep 8 2026\n\nMid-level ridging across the southern CONUS will continue to be the \nmain story through the beginning of the long term as rain chances \nremain limited over much of the area. Towards the later half of the \nweek, the surface high pressure begins to retreat to the east as a \nfront pushes into the Ohio River Valley. As the high pressure begins \nto retreat scattered showers and thunderstorms will be possible in \ncentral Georgia in the periphery of the high pressure. Although a \ncouple of storms could become strong, these storms are not expected \nto be severe and will have limited spotty rainfall. The main impact \nwill be temperatures continuing to rise to the mid 90s and heat \nindex values reaching into the low 100s again. Towards the weekend \nthe frontal system over the Ohio Valley will begin to push southward \nbefore likely stalling just north of Georgia. Despite this the \npattern in the upper levels will become more conducive to rain \nchances with the troughing to our north pushing the high pressure \noff the coast. Rain chances as a result will increase over the \nweekend with scattered showers and thunderstorms expected at this \ntime.",
